UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The divergence indicates Ridgemont has stronger short-term solvency because its higher current ratio reflects greater total current assets relative to obligations","The divergence reveals Ridgemont''s liquidity depends heavily on inventory, with its quick ratio dropping 68% when inventory is excluded, while Cascade''s minimal 10% drop confirms liquid assets dominate its working capital","The divergence is irrelevant because both companies exceed a current ratio of 2.0, which serves as the universal benchmark for adequate liquidity across all industries","The divergence indicates Cascade has a liquidity problem because its lower current ratio means it carries insufficient total current assets to cover obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the liquidity analysis framework consistent with ASC 210-10-45, Ridgemont''s current ratio of 2.8 implies $1,400,000 in current assets, but its quick ratio of 0.9 implies only $450,000 in quick assets, meaning $950,000 (68%) is inventory. This significant gap signals liquidity risk if inventory cannot convert to cash quickly, while Cascade''s narrow gap (2.1 vs. 1.9) confirms minimal inventory reliance typical of a service firm.
Wrong (A): A high current ratio inflated by illiquid inventory does not indicate strong liquidity; the quality of current assets matters more than the quantity.
