-- Q612: Financial Statement Analysis [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Includes inventory and receivables along with cash in the numerator","Uses only cash and cash equivalents divided by current liabilities","Accounts for both current and long-term debt obligations in the denominator","Measures profitability by comparing net income to current liabilities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 305-10, the cash ratio uses only cash and cash equivalents divided by current liabilities, making it the most conservative liquidity measure. It restricts the numerator to the most liquid assets, providing the strictest test of a company''s ability to meet short-term obligations immediately.
Wrong (A): Including inventory and receivables describes the current ratio, not the cash ratio.
Wrong (C): The cash ratio measures short-term liquidity using only current liabilities, not long-term debt.
Wrong (D): The cash ratio measures liquidity, not profitability; profitability ratios use net income differently.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 612;

-- Q617: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Model A forecasts $212,000 and Model B forecasts $228,000 because higher alpha weights recent actuals more heavily, making forecasts more responsive to demand changes","Both models produce identical forecasts of $220,000 because alpha only affects forecasts beginning three or more periods into the future","Model A forecasts $228,000 and Model B forecasts $212,000 because a lower alpha reacts more aggressively to the most recent actual data","Model A forecasts $240,000 and Model B forecasts $200,000 because each model ignores either the prior forecast or the actual value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT Section 301, exponential smoothing calculates F(t+1) = alpha * Actual + (1 - alpha) * Forecast. Model A: 0.3 * $240,000 + 0.7 * $200,000 = $212,000. Model B: 0.7 * $240,000 + 0.3 * $200,000 = $228,000. A higher alpha weights recent actuals more heavily, producing forecasts closer to the latest observation.
Wrong (B): Different alpha values produce different forecasts in every period, not just after three periods.
Wrong (C): This reverses the alpha-weight relationship; a lower alpha is less responsive, not more aggressive.
Wrong (D): Both models always blend the prior forecast with the actual observation; neither ignores one entirely.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 617;

-- Q619: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The prediction is reliable because an R-squared of 0.88 guarantees that 88% of future predictions will fall within an acceptable accuracy range","Extrapolation improves accuracy because the regression model becomes more precise as the input value increases beyond the original sample range","R-squared measures how well the model explains variation within the observed range, not its accuracy outside that range, so extrapolating to 15,000 hours is unreliable","The model is invalid at any data point because an R-squared below 1.00 means the regression equation has no meaningful predictive value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 270-10 cost estimation principles, R-squared of 0.88 indicates that 88% of variation in production costs is explained by machine hours within the observed range of 2,000-8,000 hours. At 15,000 hours, nearly double the maximum observed, the cost structure may change nonlinearly due to overtime premiums, equipment wear, or bottleneck effects, making the $160,000 prediction unreliable.
Wrong (A): R-squared is an in-sample statistic measuring historical fit, not a forward-looking accuracy guarantee.
Wrong (B): Extrapolation degrades reliability because the linear relationship may not hold beyond the observed data range.
Wrong (D): An R-squared of 0.88 indicates strong explanatory power within the sample; it does not need to equal 1.00 to be useful.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 619;

-- Q621: Prospective Analysis and Forecasting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Changes multiple variables simultaneously to model different plausible scenarios","Changes one variable at a time while holding all other assumptions constant","Always produces a single point estimate regardless of how many variables are tested","Is only applicable to capital budgeting decisions and cannot be used for forecasts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT Section 301 prospective financial statement standards, sensitivity analysis isolates the impact of a single variable by changing one input at a time while holding all other assumptions constant. This identifies which individual assumptions have the greatest impact on projected outcomes.
Wrong (A): Changing multiple variables simultaneously describes scenario analysis, not sensitivity analysis.
Wrong (C): Sensitivity analysis produces multiple estimates, one for each value tested on the variable being analyzed.
Wrong (D): Sensitivity analysis applies broadly to any financial forecast or projection, not just capital budgeting.$EXPL$
